<section id="rubric">
<h2>Scoring rubric</h2>
<p>Each provider gets an editorial score from 1 to 5: the weighted result of six criteria. The criteria are the questions a buyer asks when choosing a vendor, so they differ by kind of product. Identity verification, eKYC, KYC, liveness, age verification and KYB providers use the first rubric; AML screening and monitoring providers use the second. Travel Rule providers are still scored on our previous rubric until their own methodology is published. The Identity Verification ranking, Know Your Agent products, the Budget KYC ranking and the AML Software ranking have rubrics of their own, set out below: <a href="#identity-verification">Identity verification</a>, <a href="#know-your-agent">Know Your Agent</a>, <a href="#budget-kyc">Budget KYC</a> and the <a href="#aml">AML ranking</a>.</p>
<table>
<caption>Identity verification and KYC: criteria, weights and what we look at</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What we look at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Fraud defense</th><td>25%</td><td>Independent liveness test results (iBeta ISO/IEC 30107-3 level), independently tested injection attack detection, NIST face recognition listings and NFC chip reading. For KYB providers: registry checks, UBO discovery, company documents and ongoing monitoring.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Pricing and total cost</th><td>20%</td><td>Whether per-check prices are published, monthly minimums, and a free trial or free plan. A vendor that quotes only through sales scores 2 out of 5.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Coverage</th><td>15%</td><td>Countries and territories (180 or more counts as global), document types and checks that need no document, as stated by the vendor.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Platform breadth</th><td>15%</td><td>How many jobs one vendor covers: identity verification, KYB, sanctions screening, ongoing monitoring, case management, video ident, reusable ID and age checks. Specialists are measured on the modules of their own category.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Integration and time to launch</th><td>15%</td><td>Public API documentation, web, iOS and Android SDKs, a self-serve sandbox or trial, a no-code or hosted flow, and published support hours.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Trust and track record</th><td>10%</td><td>ISO/IEC 27001 or SOC 2 (more if we confirmed the certificate), government trust frameworks, data residency options, years operating, and security incidents or regulatory actions in the last 24 months.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<table>
<caption>AML screening and monitoring: criteria, weights and what we look at</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What we look at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Screening data and match quality</th><td>25%</td><td>Named sanctions lists with a stated update frequency, PEP tiers and relatives, adverse media sources, configurable fuzzy matching, and ongoing re-screening.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Pricing and total cost</th><td>20%</td><td>As above.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Investigation and audit workflow</th><td>15%</td><td>Case management, the reason for a match shown to the analyst, an audit trail, risk scoring or rules, and batch screening.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Platform breadth</th><td>15%</td><td>Screening, adverse media, transaction monitoring, case management, business screening and identity verification.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Integration and time to launch</th><td>15%</td><td>As above.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Trust and track record</th><td>10%</td><td>As above.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Each sub-score comes from a fixed table of rules, so the same evidence always gives the same sub-score, and every review shows its breakdown. If we can't find public information for a criterion, the review says "Not publicly disclosed" and that criterion's weight is shared across the others. User ratings and any commercial relationship are never part of a score.</p>
<p>Grades: Excellent from 4.5, Great 4.0 to 4.4, Very Good 3.5 to 3.9, Good 3.0 to 3.4, Fair below 3.0.</p>
</section>

<section id="identity-verification">
<h2>Identity verification rubric</h2>
<p>The <a href="/providers/identity-verification/">Identity Verification ranking</a> is ordered by a separate IDV score from the rubric below. It orders that ranking only. Each provider's review keeps its overall score.</p>
<p>We built it for one kind of buyer: regulated fintech firms (neobanks, lenders, payment and brokerage firms) and licensed gambling and betting operators in the US, UK and EU, running roughly 1,000 to 100,000 checks a month. They lose money to spoofs, deepfakes and multiple accounts, they have to read the documents of every market they are licensed in, and a regulator has to accept the check. Marketplaces, large enterprises and other buyers get their own picks in the ranking's "Best pick by use case" table. Small teams that buy mainly on price should start with the <a href="/providers/cheap-kyc/">Budget KYC ranking</a>.</p>
<table>
<caption>Identity verification: criteria, weights and what we look at</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What we look at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Fraud defense</th><td>30%</td><td>The highest iBeta ISO/IEC 30107-3 liveness level we could confirm, injection attack detection tested by an independent lab (for example under CEN/TS 18099), and NIST face recognition listings. Results from the US Department of Homeland Security's Remote Identity Validation Rally add points when the provider has disclosed them and we have matched them to the published results. A missing result never costs points, because DHS does not name the vendors it tests.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Coverage</th><td>20%</td><td>180 or more countries and territories, NFC reading of passport and ID card chips, document fraud checks described in the vendor's documentation, and a route without a document (database, bank or registry checks) in at least one market.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Pricing and total cost</th><td>15%</td><td>The same rule as the identity rubric above: published per-check prices, monthly minimums, and a free trial or free plan. A vendor that quotes only through sales scores 2 out of 5.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Integration and user flow</th><td>15%</td><td>Public API documentation, web, iOS and Android SDKs, a self-serve sandbox or trial, a no-code or hosted flow, and human review of the cases the automated check can't decide, with published hours or turnaround.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Compliance fit</th><td>15%</td><td>ISO/IEC 27001 or SOC 2 (more if we confirmed the certificate), listings on public registers we can check (UK DIATF, ISO/IEC TS 29003 under ACCS, ETSI TS 119 461, Kantara for NIST SP 800-63 IAL2), a choice of data region or an on-premises option, and security incidents or regulatory actions in the last 24 months. A framework the vendor claims but no register confirms earns no points here.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Platform breadth</th><td>5%</td><td>Only the checks these buyers add to the same contract: sanctions and PEP screening, proof of address, facial age estimation, and re-verification after onboarding, at login, account recovery or a high-risk action.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>To enter the ranking, a provider needs a published BeVerified review scored on the current rubric and a generally available product that does document verification, liveness and face match, on its own or as its own modules. A provider without an independent liveness test can still enter, and its ranking card says no independent test was found.</p>
<p>The IDV score can't tell you how many good users pass, how many are wrongly rejected or how long a check takes on your traffic. We looked for independent public tests that compare the providers in this ranking on those points and found none we could use, and we don't run our own tests yet. Vendors' pass-rate and accuracy figures use different samples, so we leave them out. Run a pilot on your own users and devices before you sign.</p>
</section>

<section id="know-your-agent">
<h2>Know Your Agent rubric</h2>
<p>Know Your Agent (KYA) products check the AI agents that act for a customer: whether a session comes from an agent, which agent it is, which verified person or business stands behind it, and what it is allowed to do. Providers with a KYA product get a separate KYA score from the rubric below. It orders the Know Your Agent ranking only. Each provider's review keeps its overall score.</p>
<table>
<caption>Know Your Agent: criteria, weights and what we look at</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What we look at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Agent detection and recognition</th><td>20%</td><td>Detection of agents that do not declare themselves, verification of signed agents (Web Bot Auth, Visa Trusted Agent Protocol, Mastercard Agent Pay) or a registry check, a trust score with reasons, and agent reputation shared across customers.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Human and business binding</th><td>20%</td><td>Whether each agent is tied to a verified person, or through KYB to a verified business, whether the person is checked again before high-risk actions, and independent evidence for that human check, such as an iBeta ISO/IEC 30107-3 letter.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Delegation and permission control</th><td>20%</td><td>Permissions by type of action, spending limits and an expiry, revocation at any time, and a check against the permission before each action runs.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Credentials and interoperability</th><td>10%</td><td>A credential in a published format such as a W3C Verifiable Credential, the agent protocols supported (MCP, A2A, AP2, ACP, Visa TAP, Mastercard Agent Pay, Web Bot Auth, OAuth), and open specifications adopted by a standards body.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Audit and monitoring</th><td>10%</td><td>A log of every agent action with the person and permission behind it, protection against tampering, monitoring after onboarding, and export for disputes and regulators.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Availability, pricing and integration</th><td>10%</td><td>Whether the product is generally available or in an open beta, a published price or free tier, public developer documentation, and a sandbox, trial or SDK.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Trust and track record</th><td>10%</td><td>The provider's trust and track record sub-score from the identity rubric, unchanged.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A provider enters the Know Your Agent ranking only when its KYA product is launched and sold, an open beta or pilot included. A framework, a white paper or a launch planned for a later date does not count: we list those providers with their status until the product ships.</p>
<p>Most KYA evidence is vendor-stated for now. No lab test or certification scheme for agent verification exists yet, and the Know Your Agent ranking says so next to the scores.</p>
</section>

<section id="budget-kyc">
<h2>Budget KYC rubric</h2>
<p>The <a href="/providers/cheap-kyc/">Budget KYC ranking</a> answers one question: what a working KYC check costs, and what quality you get for that money. Providers in it get a separate Budget score from the rubric below. It orders the Budget KYC ranking only. Each provider's review keeps its overall score.</p>
<p>A KYC check here means document verification, liveness and face match together. When a vendor sells them as separate modules, we add the module prices.</p>
<table>
<caption>Budget KYC: criteria, weights and what we look at</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What we look at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Cost at 100 and 1,000 checks a month</th><td>30%</td><td>What you pay per check at each volume on the cheapest published plan that includes the full check, after published volume tiers and with any monthly minimum or fee counted. $0.50 or less scores 5, $0.51 to $0.90 scores 4, $0.91 to $1.40 scores 3, $1.41 to $2.00 scores 2, and more than $2.00 scores 1. The two volumes count equally.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">AML screening cost</th><td>10%</td><td>What one sanctions and PEP screening per user adds at 1,000 checks a month, including the cost of moving to a higher plan if screening is only sold there. Screening included in the KYC price scores 5.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Quality for the money</th><td>35%</td><td>The fraud defense, coverage, platform breadth and integration sub-scores from the identity rubric above, at half their usual weight, scored for the KYC check whatever the provider's main category. A confirmed iBeta ISO/IEC 30107-3 letter counts here.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Commitment and entry terms</th><td>15%</td><td>The size of any monthly minimum, whether the cheapest plan needs an annual or fixed-term contract, whether you can sign up and go live without a sales call, and a trial, free tier or signup credit open to any new customer.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Price transparency</th><td>10%</td><td>Whether every part of the check is priced in public, whether AML screening is priced, whether volume tiers or the volume where custom pricing starts are published, and whether add-on fees are listed.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>To enter the Budget KYC ranking, a provider needs a published BeVerified review and a public price for the full check, visible without a login, demo or sales form and checked in the last 90 days. If it charges a monthly minimum, its own pricing page or documentation has to say the minimum covers checks; if it doesn't say, we leave the provider out. It must also sell liveness. A provider without an independent liveness test can still enter, and its ranking card says no independent test was found.</p>
<p>Prices in euros or pounds are converted to US dollars for scoring only, at the European Central Bank reference rate on the day we checked the price. Pages show prices in the currency the vendor publishes. Fees billed per user each month, such as data storage, count once for each check. One-off signup credits and trials don't lower the cost; they count under entry terms. Price estimates from marketplaces and buyer data sites never make a provider eligible and never enter the Budget score.</p>
<p>The Budget score works from list prices. It can't see discounts you negotiate, checks you pay for twice when a user retries, VAT on your invoice, or how many good users a cheap check turns away. Ask about these before you sign.</p>
</section>

<section id="aml">
<h2>AML ranking rubric</h2>
<p>The <a href="/providers/aml/">AML Software ranking</a> is ordered by a separate AML score from the rubric below. It orders that ranking only. Each provider's review keeps its overall score, and the <a href="/providers/aml/banks/">AML Software for Banks</a> ranking keeps its own list.</p>
<p>We built it for one kind of buyer: fintechs, e-money institutions, payment firms and crypto-asset service providers in the EU, UK and US, screening roughly 1,000 to 500,000 customers with a compliance team of one to ten people. They have to catch every name on the sanctions lists they are bound by, see new designations quickly, keep false alerts low enough to clear, and show a regulator how the tool is set up. The criteria follow what supervisors ask of screening systems (the UK FCA, OFAC, the New York DFS and the European Banking Authority) and the screening guidance of the Wolfsberg Group of global banks.</p>
<table>
<caption>AML ranking: criteria, weights and what we look at</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What we look at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Sanctions, PEP and media data</th><td>25%</td><td>Whether the vendor names the OFAC SDN list, the EU consolidated financial sanctions list, the UK Sanctions List and the UN Security Council consolidated list, how many hours it says list changes take to arrive (24 hours or less scores best), PEP data with tiers or relatives and close associates, adverse media from named or counted sources, and whether it says where its data comes from.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Match quality controls</th><td>20%</td><td>A match threshold you can set, matching across transliterations and non-Latin scripts, date of birth, nationality or ID numbers used to rule out a match, a way to stop a cleared match from coming back on every re-screen, and the reason for a match shown on the alert.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Calibration, testing and audit</th><td>15%</td><td>An audit trail of every alert and decision, case management with a second reviewer or approval step, public documentation of how the matching settings work, reports on alerts, true matches and list update times, and help with validating the setup. Security incidents and regulatory actions in the last 24 months cost points here.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Ongoing and transfer monitoring</th><td>15%</td><td>Automatic re-screening of your customers when lists change or at least daily, transaction monitoring rules, screening of payments or crypto transfers before they go through, and customer risk scores that update on new data.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Pricing and total cost</th><td>15%</td><td>The same rule as the rubrics above, with name screening as the core product: published prices per screen or per monitored customer, monthly minimums, and a free trial or free plan. A vendor that quotes only through sales scores 2 out of 5.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Integration</th><td>10%</td><td>Public API documentation, a self-serve sandbox or trial, batch screening by file upload or SFTP, webhooks for monitoring alerts, and a dashboard where analysts can search and review cases without code.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A feature we can't find in the vendor's public material earns no points. Its weight doesn't move to other criteria, so keeping documentation private never lifts a score. Pricing is the one exception and follows the general rule above. Vendor figures such as "90% fewer false positives", and counts of lists or profiles that don't name the lists, never enter the score.</p>
<p>To enter the ranking, a provider needs a published BeVerified review scored on the current rubric and a generally available product that screens names against sanctions and PEP data and keeps monitoring customers after onboarding. A platform whose main product is something else, for example identity verification with a screening module, also has to describe its screening in its own documentation well enough to earn at least 2 points in both the data and match quality criteria. A provider that doesn't qualify is left out of the ranking, not scored low.</p>
<p>The AML score can't tell you how many real hits a system misses, how many false alerts it raises on your customers, or how fast a new designation reaches it in practice. We don't run our own screening tests yet. Before you sign, screen a sample of your own customers, add known listed names in several spellings and scripts, ask how many hours a new OFAC, EU or UK designation takes to appear, and ask for the calibration documentation you would show your regulator.</p>
</section>

<section id="evidence">
<h2>Where the evidence comes from</h2>
<p>Every fact on a review carries one of four labels, so you can see how much weight to give it:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Independent:</strong> a test lab, a government register or a public benchmark, for example an iBeta test letter, a NIST evaluation or a certification register.</li>
<li><strong>Vendor-stated:</strong> the vendor's own website, documentation, trust centre or pricing page.</li>
<li><strong>User-reported:</strong> review platforms such as G2, Capterra and Gartner Peer Insights. We show these ratings separately; they are not part of our score.</li>
<li><strong>Hands-on:</strong> our own test of the product.</li>
</ul>
<p>Numbered sources at the end of each review link to the page we used and the date we accessed it. Prices show the month we last checked them, and third-party price estimates are marked as such.</p>
</section>

<section id="hands-on">
<h2>Hands-on testing</h2>
<p>Most reviews are currently based on public evidence. When we test a product ourselves, the review gets a "Manually tested" badge and a Hands-on test section with the test date and results. Reviews without that section have not been tested hands-on.</p>
</section>

<section id="freshness">
<h2>How we keep reviews current</h2>
<p>We aim to re-check each review every 90 days. The "Last verified" date changes only after we have actually re-checked the provider's pricing, coverage and certifications, not when we edit wording.</p>
</section>

<section id="ranking">
<h2>How rankings are ordered</h2>
<p>Category and country rankings are ordered by each provider's score. When two providers have the same score, we keep the order they had before the tie. Providers we list but haven't reviewed in full yet appear after the reviewed ones, without a score. A provider whose score is still on the previous rubric is left out of rankings until it is re-scored.</p>
</section>

<section id="limitations">
<h2>What the score does not measure</h2>
<ul>
<li>Public evidence can't show false rejection rates, completion rates, manual review rates, false positive rates in name matching or latency on your own traffic. Vendor figures use different methods and samples. Test these in a proof of concept on your own users.</li>
<li>We don't load-test vendor APIs, so a score says nothing about performance at production scale.</li>
<li>We only see public regulatory actions and disclosures, not private examinations.</li>
<li>A score is an editorial opinion, not a legal opinion. Choosing a high-scoring provider does not by itself meet a regulator's expectations for vendor due diligence.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="disclosure">
<h2>How we make money</h2>
<p>BeVerified may earn a commission when a reader signs up with a provider through a link on this site. Payment never changes scores or ranking order.</p>
<p>Some providers pay for the link to their website. Those links carry a visible "Sponsored link" label next to the "Visit site" button. Links to providers that don't pay are marked <code>rel="nofollow"</code>. A paid link doesn't buy a review, a score, an award or a position in a ranking: paying providers are checked against the same rubric and the same sources as everyone else.</p>
